        "titolo_orig": "Trump taps housing regulator Bill Pulte as acting intelligence chief",
        "testo_it": "Donald Trump sceglie Bill Pulte, privo di esperienza nel settore, come capo ad interim dell'intelligence USA.",
        "priorita": "Alta",
        "id_gruppo": 2,
        "link": "https://www.scmp.com/news/us/diplomacy/article/3355746/trump-taps-housing-regulator-bill-pulte-acting-intelligence-chief?utm_source=rss_feed",
        "orario": "17:43",
        "timestamp": 1780415015,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement United States US Diplomacy Trump taps housing regulator Bill Pulte as acting intelligence chief The close ally with no known intelligence experience will oversee 18 US agencies while keeping his current role regulating mortgage market 3 -MIN READ 3 -MIN 1 Listen Teresa Elena Frontado in Washington Published: 11:43pm, 2 Jun 2026 Updated: 12:36am, 3 Jun 2026 US President Donald Trump has named federal housing regulator Bill Pulte as acting director of national intelligence, entrusting oversight of America’s intelligence agencies to a close political ally whose career has been rooted in housing finance rather than national security. Trump announced the appointment on Tuesday, saying Pulte would replace Tulsi Gabbard, who stepped down last month after citing her husband’s cancer diagnosis. The move places the 38-year-old director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency at the helm of the nation’s intelligence community that oversees 18 agencies, including the CIA and National Security Agency, while allowing him to retain his existing responsibilities regulating the mortgage market. Advertisement The appointment is likely to draw scrutiny because it places a Trump loyalist with no known intelligence background atop the group responsible for assessing threats from countries including China, Russia, Iran and North Korea. Yet Pulte has increasingly framed parts of his work at the housing regulator through a national security lens, including investigations he said involved alleged Chinese and North Korean operatives. Advertisement In a post on social media announcing his appointment, Trump praised Pulte’s management of government-backed mortgage giants Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
